          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why is aluminium used so widely in Indian elevation design?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Aluminium is light enough to install without heavy structural support, does not rust the way untreated steel does, can be extruded, folded, perforated or cast into an enormous range of profiles, and accepts factory-applied coatings that hold colour and finish for decades. For a country with intense UV exposure, coastal salinity and a demanding monsoon, those properties matter more than they might in a milder climate."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the difference between PVDF, powder coating and anodising for aluminium?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "PVDF (polyvinylidene fluoride) coating is the premium standard, offering strong colour retention and chalking resistance under intense UV, commonly carrying 15 to 30 year warranties. Powder coating is more affordable and performs well for a shorter service life, typically suited to interior-facing or less sun-exposed applications. Anodising is an electrochemical process rather than a paint coating, producing a distinctive metallic finish with excellent durability where a natural metal look is desired."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much does an aluminium elevation design cost in India?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Engineered aluminium systems, including solid panel, fluted, cassette and perforated formats with a proper substructure and PVDF finish, generally range from ₹800 to ₹1,800 per square foot installed. Fully custom parametric aluminium systems sit at the higher end, commonly ₹1,500 to ₹2,500 per square foot or more, reflecting the design and fabrication effort involved."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What causes aluminium facades to fail before their rated lifespan?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The failures that do occur almost always trace back to one of two causes: a lower grade coating that was not rated for the intensity of Indian UV exposure, or poor detailing at joints and fixing points that allowed water ingress behind the panel. This is why asking a supplier specifically about coating grade, warranty terms and joint detailing matters more than comparing headline prices alone."
          }
        }
